# flex_heptane.wsp5 — P0 literal revision of the carry of D2 sha256:b6bc5a9a…0f2c, spec flex_heptane-wsp-v5
# Author: claude-fable-5-2 (Anthropic). DATA-INFORMED, not zero-shot. The carried numbers
# (flex_heptane.wsp2 D2) with four laws revised from the physical mechanism: a physics dossier on
# these droplets (docs/wsp/research/phys_flex_heptane.md), re-derived independently on the TRAIN runs
# (docs/wsp/feedback/verify_phys_flex_heptane.md), and the camera times at which the hot flame ended
# on train (docs/wsp/feedback/flex_flame_traces_train.md). Every number typed by the author; no fit,
# no search. The author had seen the aggregate validation tables of the earlier candidates and the
# CONDITIONS of the validation runs (inputs only); no validation outcome was read and nothing below
# was chosen from a score. The flame cameras are not read by this candidate.
#
# c = (c_o2, c_co2, c_he, c_logp, c_logd0), as in the carried model.

# =====================================================================================================
# 1. The cool-flame burning rate does not depend on oxygen or pressure
# =====================================================================================================
# A cool flame burns at a fixed flame temperature (600-800 K), so its rate is set by the gas
# conductivity and not by how much oxygen there is. Train, twelve runs with a sustained cool flame:
# 0.32-0.36 mm2/s (median 0.345), no trend beyond 0.02 in oxygen (15-24 %), CO2 (0-25 %) or pressure
# (0.7 against 1 atm); droplets of 3.3 mm and more sit 0.015 above smaller ones. The carried law had
# +0.2 per 5 % of oxygen and +0.1 per e-fold of pressure and gave 0.41-0.44 for the 0.7 atm runs,
# which burned at 0.34-0.36. Typed: 0.345 mm2/s at 3 mm (0.325 at the 2 mm reference), size exponent
# 0.15, no oxygen, CO2 or pressure term. Helium raises the conductivity: theory 1.21 times at 15 %,
# the one train run (H19H101, whose sheet and table disagree on its atmosphere) 1.25 times: +0.2 per
# 20 % of helium (1.16 at 15 %). Above 1 atm a hotter "warm" flame takes over and burns faster
# (published numerical work; 0.49 mm2/s in the one train run at 2 atm): +0.5 per e-fold of pressure
# above 1e5 Pa, which gives 0.50 at 2 atm. One run, and it is the run the number comes from.
cool.k.b0 = -1.125
cool.k.skip[0:5] = [0.0, 0.0, 0.2, 0.0, 0.15]
cool.k.W1[0, 3] = 1.0
cool.k.b1[0] = 0.0
cool.k.w2[0] = 0.5

# =====================================================================================================
# 2. Where the cool flame ends: a law in the oxygen partial pressure, with a limit
# =====================================================================================================
# Published theory: the cool flame ends where the residence time falls to its chemical limit, and the
# diameter there scales as ([O2] - b)^(-1/2), diverging at an oxygen limit b. Train, twelve plateaus
# re-measured by a second reader: D_ce/D0 = 0.94 at 0.140 atm of oxygen, 0.65-0.68 at 0.151,
# 0.57-0.61 at 0.160-0.168 (one of them at 0.7 atm and 23 % oxygen: the variable is the partial
# pressure, not the mole fraction), 0.47-0.52 at 0.180, 0.39-0.43 at 0.190. The end diameter scales
# with the initial diameter (exponent 0.95 on four same-atmosphere pairs, range 0.8-1.25), which the
# quasi-steady theory does not give and the carried law had as 0.8. With g0 = 0.47 at 0.18 atm and
# b = 0.12 atm the form gives 0.81, 0.65, 0.58, 0.53, 0.47, 0.44: within 13 % on eleven plateaus;
# the flame at 0.140 atm sits higher still (a limit of 0.125 would fit it; 0.12 is the lower end of
# what the plateaus allow). The carried law was linear in oxygen and gave 0.56 where 0.94 was measured.
# Helium: (1 + x_He), from the one run at 15 % (0.455 measured, 0.45 by the form).
ce.on = 1.0
ce.g0 = 0.47
ce.beta_atm = 0.12
ce.p_ref_atm = 0.18
ce.c_he = 1.0

# =====================================================================================================
# 3. A flame born below its end diameter dies away; the helium patch goes
# =====================================================================================================
# The carried model forced "no cool flame" above 15 % helium with a unit of its own, and then let the
# droplet evaporate like one that had left a cool flame at 1.7 mm. In 30-35 % helium at 0.7 atm the
# oxygen partial pressure is 0.14-0.15 atm: by the law above the cool flame would end at or above the
# diameter at which the hot flame is lost. It is born past its limit and decays. Train, three runs of
# one session, D2 rate after the visible flame is lost: 0.51-0.59 mm2/s in the first second,
# 0.40-0.52 in the second, 0.29-0.35 in the third, 0.22-0.25 over 3-5 s, 0.11-0.15 over 5-8 s; the
# carried unlit law gives 0.14, 0.12, 0.10, 0.08, 0.055. The difference starts at the full cool-flame
# rate of that atmosphere (0.45-0.50 by section 1) and falls by e every 3.5 s.
# So: the helium unit of the cool-flame probability is switched off (the end-diameter law decides),
# and a flame of zero length is followed by the full cool rate decaying with 3.5 s.
cool.p.w2[1] = 0.0
dy.frac = 1.0
dy.tau_s = 3.5

# =====================================================================================================
# 4. Helium runs the radiative clock faster
# =====================================================================================================
# The cameras show when the hot flame ended on train. Against them the carried clock is right for N2
# and CO2 (17 runs inside its bands) and 1.5 times late in 30-35 % helium (flame held 2-3 s too long;
# one run at D0/D_crit 1.30 lost its flame at clock 0.32 where the band says 0.41-0.80). The methanol
# model has had -0.22 per 20 % of helium since the third generation, from its own three runs, and the
# far field of oxygen builds up faster in helium (physics: -0.17). The same term here: 0.68 at 35 %.
# The third generation had declined it for heptane for want of flame times; the cameras supply them.
rad.skip[0:6] = [0.0, 0.0, -0.22, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]

# Sections 3 and 4 belong together. Scored one at a time on train each makes the helium runs worse:
# the carried model holds the hot flame too long and then lets the droplet evaporate too slowly, and
# the two errors cancel. Together they are right for the right reason (train mean 45.9 against 46.2
# micrometres, median 20.3 against 21.8, with section 2).
#
# What is NOT changed, and why. The evaporation of an unlit droplet stays the carried law: the
# closed form "a hot droplet evaporates on its stored heat" gives the right rate for the first two
# seconds after a cool flame, but its decay time grows as D^2 and the train runs do not show that
# (the independent check). The critical diameter and the hot burning rate stay: the physics
# reproduces the typed laws within 10 % in N2 and CO2 up to 30 % and does no better at 0.7 or 2 atm.
